  "worked_solution": "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it\u2019s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}^{\u2032} & {(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1)}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)-(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}\u2061x-({cos}^{2}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one\u00a0}x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$",
